Squark: msvcrt: math_error drops FEX's JIT FPCR bits before the matherr handler

FEX patch 0044 lets x64 code call the C runtime's math functions (cos, sin, atan2, fmod, pow, ...) without FPCR
leaving the JIT's mode: their arithmetic is scalar, and AH and NEP do not change a scalar result's value. Each of
them reports errors through math_error, which calls the program's matherr handler (any code, native or x64) and
sets errno. math_error now drops AH, NEP and the trap enables (0x9f06) first when any is set, like the debug output
entry points of patch 0070; the rest of the call, back in the math function included, runs with the native FPCR, and
FEX writes its own back when the call returns to x64 code. One mrs and a branch for every other caller.

fpcrscan.py (squark-app runtime/fex) ends a path at math_error for this reason.

diff --git a/dlls/msvcrt/math.c b/dlls/msvcrt/math.c
--- a/dlls/msvcrt/math.c
+++ b/dlls/msvcrt/math.c
@@ -123,6 +123,15 @@
 {
     struct _exception exception = {type, (char *)name, arg1, arg2, retval};
 
+#ifdef __arm64ec__
+    /* FEX can call a math function with its JIT's FPCR bits still set (AH, NEP, the trap enables it arms from
+     * MXCSR): scalar arithmetic gives the same values either way. The matherr handler called below is any
+     * code, so the rest of this call runs with the native bits; FEX writes its own back on return to x64. */
+    unsigned __int64 fpcr;
+    __asm__ __volatile__( "mrs %0, fpcr" : "=r" (fpcr) );
+    if (fpcr & 0x9f06) __asm__ __volatile__( "msr fpcr, %0" : : "r" (fpcr & ~(unsigned __int64)0x9f06) );
+#endif
+
     TRACE("(%d, %s, %g, %g, %g)\n", type, debugstr_a(name), arg1, arg2, retval);
 
     if (MSVCRT_default_matherr_func && MSVCRT_default_matherr_func(&exception))
